From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:57:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Nest: Fix over-read when copying attribute data

Triggered by storing an attribute list with non-embedded data whose
length is not a multiple of the CPU alignment, e.g. on every BGP
connection, which stores its socket info (struct bgp_conn_sk_ad) as
protocol state.

ea_list_copy() copies ADATA_SIZE(length) bytes of every non-embedded
attribute, which is sizeof(struct adata) + length rounded up to the CPU
alignment. The source is often only sizeof(struct adata) + length long,
e.g. lp_alloc_adata() does not round up, or the struct bgp_conn_sk_ad
which bgp_setup_sk() passes from the stack. ASan reports a stack buffer
overflow there for every BGP connection.

Copy just the data and zero the padding.
---
--- a/nest/rt-attr.c
+++ b/nest/rt-attr.c
@@ -1235,11 +1235,14 @@ ea_list_copy(ea_list *n, ea_list *o, uint elen)
       eattr *a = &n->attrs[i];
       if (!a->undef && !(a->type & EAF_EMBEDDED))
 	{
+	  /* The source may be just as long as the data, without the padding */
+	  unsigned len = sizeof(struct adata) + a->u.ptr->length;
 	  unsigned size = ADATA_SIZE(a->u.ptr->length);
 	  ASSERT_DIE(adpos + size <= elen);
 
 	  struct adata *d = ((void *) n) + adpos;
-	  memcpy(d, a->u.ptr, size);
+	  memcpy(d, a->u.ptr, len);
+	  memset((void *) d + len, 0, size - len);
 	  a->u.ptr = d;
 
 	  adpos += size;
